  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Phasors at Initial Time",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a sinusoid's amplitude and phase into the fixed $t=0 complex phasor that anchors its later rotation and axis projections$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Phasors at Initial Time",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Phasors at Initial Time",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A phasor at initial time is the fixed complex snapshot of a sinusoid at $t=0$. For a sinusoid written as $x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$, the amplitude $A$ becomes the phasor's magnitude and the phase $\\phi$ becomes its angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the zero-phase case first: if $\\phi=0$, the initial phasor sits on the positive real axis. In that base case, the snapshot is just the positive real number $A$, so the learner can see directly how amplitude sets the length before any phase rotation is added.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=Ae^{j\\phi}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an arbitrary phase, the same initial phasor is written in exponential form as $X_0=Ae^{j\\phi}$. That representation keeps the magnitude in $A$ and the angle in $\\phi$, and later lessons will use that same phasor to show how rotation and axis projections come from the same fixed starting point.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=A\\cos\\phi+jA\\sin\\phi",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In rectangular form, the real part is $A\\cos\\phi$ and the imaginary part is $A\\sin\\phi$. So the initial phasor is the same object in three views: a sinusoid's amplitude and phase, a complex exponential $Ae^{j\\phi}$, and a rectangular complex number with real and imaginary parts set by $\\phi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Phasors at Initial Time",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Writing an Initial Phasor with Zero Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=6\\cos(\\omega t)$, write its initial phasor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is zero phase, so the sinusoid is already in the simplest base case. The amplitude is $6$, and at $t=0$ that amplitude becomes the phasor's magnitude while the phase stays at angle $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=6\\cos(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the phase is $0$, the initial phasor sits on the positive real axis. Write the phasor in exponential form first, using the amplitude as the magnitude and $0$ as the angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=6e^{j0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $e^{j0}=1$, the initial phasor simplifies to a real number. The result is the complex snapshot $6$, which is the same phasor written on the positive real axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=6",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Phasors at Initial Time",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Writing an Initial Phasor with Positive Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=8\\cos(\\omega t+\\pi/3)$, write its initial phasor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is positive phase, so the only change from the zero-phase case is the angle of the phasor. The amplitude is $8$, and the phase $+\\pi/3$ becomes the phasor angle at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=8\\cos(\\omega t+\\pi/3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the initial phasor in exponential form by keeping $8$ as the magnitude and using the positive phase as the angle. A positive angle places the phasor above the positive real axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=8e^{j\\pi/3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If you expand that phasor into rectangular form, the real part is $8\\cos(\\pi/3)$ and the imaginary part is $8\\sin(\\pi/3)$. That confirms the same initial phasor sits in the upper half-plane and keeps the phase sign visible in the complex snapshot.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=8\\cos(\\pi/3)+j8\\sin(\\pi/3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Phasors at Initial Time",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing an Initial Phasor with Negative Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=10\\cos(\\omega t-\\pi/4)$, write its initial phasor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is negative phase, so the only change from the positive-phase case is the sign of the angle. The amplitude is $10$, and the phase $-\\pi/4$ becomes the phasor angle at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=10\\cos(\\omega t-\\pi/4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the initial phasor in exponential form by keeping $10$ as the magnitude and using the negative phase as the angle. A negative angle places the phasor below the positive real axis.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=10e^{-j\\pi/4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If you expand that phasor into rectangular form, the real part is $10\\cos(\\pi/4)$ and the imaginary part is $-10\\sin(\\pi/4)$. That confirms the same initial phasor sits in the lower half-plane and keeps the negative sign visible in the complex snapshot.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=10\\cos(\\pi/4)-j10\\sin(\\pi/4)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Phasors at Initial Time",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Converting an Initial Phasor to Rectangular Form",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert the initial phasor $8e^{j\\pi/3}$ to rectangular form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the initial phasor in exponential form and use $Ae^{j\\phi}=A\\cos\\phi+jA\\sin\\phi$ to read off the real and imaginary parts.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=8e^{j\\pi/3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Rewrite the exponential with cosine and sine, keeping the same amplitude $A=8$ and phase $\\phi=\\pi/3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=8\\cos(\\pi/3)+j8\\sin(\\pi/3)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now evaluate the trig values: $\\cos(\\pi/3)=1/2$ and $\\sin(\\pi/3)=\\sqrt{3}/2$. That gives the rectangular form with a real part of $4$ and an imaginary part of $4\\sqrt{3}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_0=4+j4\\sqrt{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
